### What happened?

| Query | Expected | Actual |
| --- | --- | --- |
| { true \|\| .does_not_exist } | 3 spans | 0 spans |
| { status = error \|\| .does_not_exist } | 1 span | 0 spans |
| { .cache.hit \|\| .does_not_exist } | 1 span | 0 spans |
| { .cache.hit \|\| .does_not_exist } over 2,000 spans where .cache.hit = true | 2,000 spans | 0 spans |
| { .cache.hit \|\| .cache.hit } control | expected matches | correct |

### What did you expect to happen?

Per documented TraceQL boolean semantics, a missing attribute should behave as a false/unresolved operand, never as a reason to discard a true sibling in a boolean OR:

{ true || .anything } should match every span.
{ .cache.hit || .optional_attr } should match every span where cache.hit is boolean true, regardless of whether optional_attr is absent.
{ status = error || .optional_attr } should match every error span.


### Did this work before?

Unkown

